     1,
     "Hey,"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<br><br>On Friday, 12 July 2013 23:07:19 UTC+2, adam.bickford  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">Hello ggplot mavens,</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">I have four time series with the following structure:</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">WEEK a date variable indicating the last day of a week (on the x-axis), </font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">OUTCOME a numeric outcome variable (on the y-axis), and </font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">CAT a factor variable that differentiates the 4 variable series.</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\" color=\"#000000\" face=\"Calibri\"> </font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">I want to produce a plot with a smoothed lowess line for each level of CAT.  I can produce the line plot with LINETYPE differentiated by CAT, but I don’t see how to fit the smoothed line.</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">I’d appreciate any suggestions how to do this.</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\" color=\"#000000\" face=\"Calibri\"> </font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">Thanks in advance </font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">AB</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\" color=\"#000000\" face=\"Calibri\"> </font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">Sample code producing the line graph below:</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">x1&lt;- ggplot(df, aes(x=WEEJ, y=OUTCOME, linetype=CAT)) +</font></font></font></p></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div>You should add group=CAT to your aesthetic and maybe colour=CAT, too, so that you can distinguish them nicely. <br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  geom_line() +</font></font></font></p></blockquote><div>You possibly want to remove the call to geom_line() since you have data points that you want to fit. I&#39;d use:<br>    geom_point() +<br>    geom_smooth(method=&quot;loess&quot;) + <br></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  scale_y_continuous(&quot;Outcome&quot;) +</font></font></font> </p></blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  scale_x_date(&quot;Week ending&quot;, breaks = date_breaks(&quot;1 week&quot;), labels= date_format(format = &quot;%m/%d&quot;)) +</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  scale_linetype_manual(name= &quot;Category&quot;, breaks=c(&quot;LE 13&quot;,&quot;14 to 20&quot;,&quot;21 to 26&quot;, &quot;GT 26&quot;), values = c(10,8,5,1)) +</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  theme(legend.position=&quot;bottom&quot;,axis.text.x=element_text(angle=-70, hjust=0)) +</font></font></font></p>\r\n<p style=\"MARGIN:0in 0in 0pt\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font size=\"3\"><font color=\"#000000\"><font face=\"Calibri\">  ggtitle (&quot;Outcome by Category&quot;)</font></font></font></p></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div> <font color=\"#000000\"><font size=\"3\"><font face=\"Calibri\"><br><font size=\"3\">I Hope that&#39;s <font size=\"3\">gives you w<font size=\"3\">hat you w<font size=\"3\">anted. You should also browse the excellent docs at <a href=\"http://docs.ggplot2.org/current/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://docs.ggplot2.org/current/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372445229000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1rnICn2WH5OMrlgNA9aUl_\">http://docs.ggplot2.org/current/</a> for more inspiration.<br><br><font size=\"3\">Best,<br><font size=\"3\">Moritz</font><br></font></font></font></font></font></font></font></font></div><div> </div><div> </div>"
    ]
   ]
